## Wrong, and just as sure

<At levels="simple">

As <ChapterRef to="quirks-and-limits" /> showed, a model can make up a fact and state it as
confidently as a true one. Bigger models do it less, but nothing in a reply marks the made-up
parts. In 2023, two New York lawyers filed a court brief citing six court decisions that ChatGPT
had invented. When they asked it whether the cases were real, it said they were. The judge fined
them &#36;5,000.<Cite id="mata2023" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

As <ChapterRef to="quirks-and-limits" /> showed, a model writes likely text, not checked facts.
When it doesn't know an answer, it can still write a confident one: a <GlossaryPopover term="hallucination">hallucination</GlossaryPopover>. Bigger,
tuned models do this less often, but nothing in a reply marks which parts are made up. In 2023, two
New York lawyers filed a court brief citing six decisions that ChatGPT had invented, quotes and
all. When they asked it whether the cases were real, it said they were. The judge fined them
&#36;5,000 and ordered them to write to each judge falsely named as an author.<Cite id="mata2023" />

</At>

## Ask it ten times

<At levels="simple">

One way to catch a guess is to ask the same question several times. If the model knows the answer,
it gives the same one each time; if it's guessing, the answers scatter.<Cite id="manakul2023" />
Below, a small chat model answered each of 11 questions ten times. Three of them ask about a book,
a chemist, and a town invented for this chapter.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

With sampling on (<ChapterRef to="probability-and-sampling" />), the same question can get a
different answer each time, and that is useful. When a model knows a fact, most of its probability
sits on one answer, so the samples agree. When it's guessing, the probability is spread thin, so
they scatter. SelfCheckGPT turned this into a check that needs nothing but the model itself: sample
several answers and see whether they agree.<Cite id="manakul2023" /> Below, Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct
answered each of 11 questions ten times, sampled with its usual settings. Three of them ask about a
book, a chemist, and a town invented for this chapter.

</At>

<SampleCheck />

<At levels="simple">

Asked who wrote a book that doesn't exist, the model names nine different authors. On the questions
it knows, all ten answers match. A check that trusts an answer only when 8 of 10 agree flags 4 of
the 5 wrong answers and none of the right ones. It misses the Tour de France: all ten answers say
Eddy Merckx, who won it five times, but not in 1987. Agreement shows the model is sure, not that
it's right.

A common tip is to turn the temperature down (<ChapterRef to="probability-and-sampling" />). At
temperature 0 the model gets the same six right and the same five wrong. For the invented book it
now says "Helen Macdonald" every time, and nothing shows that it's guessing.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Asked who wrote a book that doesn't exist, the model names nine different authors in ten tries. On
the six questions it gets right, all ten samples agree. A harness that passes an answer on only
when at least 8 of 10 samples agree flags 4 of the 5 wrong answers and none of the right ones. The
one it misses is the Tour de France: all ten samples say Eddy Merckx, who won it five times, but
not in 1987, when Stephen Roche did. When a model has learned something wrong, it says it every
time, so agreement shows the model is sure, not that it's right. The check also costs ten calls
instead of one; <ChapterRef to="routing" /> got a cheaper signal from the model's confidence in a
single answer.

A common tip is to turn the temperature down. At temperature 0, taking the likeliest token every
time (<ChapterRef to="probability-and-sampling" />), the model gets the same six right and the same
five wrong. For the invented book it says "Helen Macdonald", a name none of the ten samples gave,
and it says it every time. A lower temperature doesn't make a guess true; it makes the guess
repeatable, and hides the scatter that gave it away. The line under the table shows each answer
at temperature 0.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### Measuring the scatter

The spread can be put in one number: the entropy of the answers. If $n_i$ of the $N$ samples give
answer $i$,

$$
H = -\sum_i \frac{n_i}{N} \ln \frac{n_i}{N}
$$

which is 0 when all ten agree and $\ln 10 \approx 2.30$ when all ten differ. The invented book
scores 2.16; the 1995 Nobel Prize, with 6 votes for Saul Bellow, 3 for Svetlana Alexievich, and 1
for "Aleksievich", scores 0.90. Counting the two spellings as one answer brings it down to 0.67.
*Semantic entropy* does exactly that: it groups the samples by meaning, using a second model to
judge whether two answers say the same thing, before taking the entropy.<Cite id="kuhn2023" /> The
widget only groups answers that match word for word.

</At>

## Letting it say "I don't know"

<At levels="simple">

Tick the box to add one sentence to the system prompt: if you're not sure, say "I don't know". Now
the model declines every time for the invented book and town, and keeps all its right answers. But
it still makes up a year for the invented chemist, and it still says Merckx.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Tick the box to add one sentence to the system prompt: if you are not sure of the answer, say "I
don't know". The model now declines all ten times for the invented book and the invented town, and
half the time for the 1995 Nobel Prize. It keeps all six right answers. But it still makes up a
prize year for the invented chemist, and it still says Merckx: a model can only decline when
something in it registers that it doesn't know.

</At>

## Why guessing pays

<At levels="simple">

Why do models guess at all? Training rewards likely text, true or not, and facts that appear in few
training documents are the ones models most often get wrong.<Cite id="kandpal2023" /> And most
tests of models are graded like school exams: a right answer scores a point, and a wrong answer or
"I don't know" scores nothing. A guess never scores less than admitting doubt, so a model tuned to
do well on those tests learns to guess.<Cite id="kalai2025" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Part of the cause is training: as in <ChapterRef to="quirks-and-limits" />, the loss rewards likely
text, true or not, and a fact the training text mentions only once leaves no pattern to learn from.
Rarity matters well beyond once. Across several question sets and models up to 176 billion
weights, accuracy on a question rose with the number of training documents about its subject, and
retrieving documents at answer time made the count matter less.<Cite id="kandpal2023" />
That fits the widget: all four well-known answers come back right, and two of the four less
well-known ones do.

Researchers at OpenAI and Georgia Tech argue that the rest comes from how models are tested. Most
benchmarks grade
like a multiple-choice exam: 1 point for a right answer and 0 for a wrong one, and "I don't know"
also scores 0. Under that scoring a guess never does worse than admitting doubt, so a model tuned to
climb those leaderboards learns to guess. Of the ten widely reported benchmarks they looked at, nine
gave no credit at all for "I don't know".<Cite id="kalai2025" /> Their proposed fix is to score
mistakes below "I don't know", and to say so in the instructions.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### Where guessing stops paying

Their proposal tells the model: answer only if you are more than $t$ confident, since a mistake
costs $t/(1-t)$ points, a right answer earns 1, and "I don't know" earns 0. If the model's chance
of being right is $p$, answering is worth

$$
p - (1 - p)\,\frac{t}{1 - t}
$$

points on average, which is above 0 exactly when $p > t$. With $t = 0.9$ a mistake costs 9 points,
so only an answer that is more than 90% likely to be right is worth giving. Ordinary exam grading
is $t = 0$, where any guess beats "I don't know".<Cite id="kalai2025" />

They give a bound for training too. For facts with no pattern to them, like people's birthdays, a
base model should be expected to get at least as large a share wrong as the share of those facts
that appear exactly once in its training text: if 20% of birthdays appear once, expect at least
20% of its answers about birthdays to be wrong.<Cite id="kalai2025" />

</At>

## Made-up names that attackers can use

<At levels="simple">

Made-up answers can also open a door for attackers. Models that write code sometimes tell you to
install software packages that don't exist: in one study, at least 5% of the packages suggested by
commercial models and 22% from open models were made up.<Cite id="spracklen2025" /> Anyone can
publish a package under an unused name, so an attacker can publish harmful code under a name a model
invented, and wait for someone to install it.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Made-up answers can also open a door for attackers. When models that write code suggest software
packages to install, some of the packages don't exist. Across 16 models and 576,000 code samples,
on average at least 5.2% of the packages suggested by commercial models and 21.7% of those from
open-source models were made up: 205,474 different invented names in all.<Cite id="spracklen2025" />
Package registries let anyone publish under an unused name. So an attacker can publish harmful code
under a name a model invented, and wait for a person, or an agent running install commands
(<ChapterRef to="the-agent-loop" />), to install it.

</At>

## What helps

<At levels="simple">

- **Give it the facts.** A model that answers from text put into its prompt
  (<ChapterRef to="search-and-memory" />) has less to guess. Even so, legal research tools that look
  up cases before answering still made things up 17% to 33% of the time.<Cite id="magesh2024" />
- **Let it decline**, as the box above does.
- **Ask more than once** when a wrong answer would be costly.
- **Check one claim at a time, in a new chat.** A model that has given a wrong answer tends to
  defend it with more wrong claims, but asked about each claim on its own, it often spots the
  mistake.<Cite id="zhang2023snowball" />
- **Check what can be checked**, with code or a person: that a court case exists, that a package is
  real.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

No single fix removes the problem, but several help:

- **Put the facts in the prompt.** A model answering from text the harness retrieved
  (<ChapterRef to="search-and-memory" />) has less to guess. It still isn't immune: in a
  preregistered study, legal research tools from LexisNexis and Thomson Reuters that retrieve cases
  before answering, some advertised as free of hallucinations, made things up 17% to 33% of the
  time.<Cite id="magesh2024" />
- **Let it decline**, as the system prompt above does, and when you test it
  (<ChapterRef to="testing-agents" />), don't score "I don't know" like a wrong answer.
- **Ask more than once** when a wrong answer would be costly, and treat disagreement as a warning.
  Don't turn the temperature down to make answers look consistent.
- **Check claims one at a time, in a fresh call.** Once a model has committed to a wrong answer, it
  tends to justify it with more false claims, ones it can recognize as false on their own: shown its
  own wrong claims in a separate session, ChatGPT spotted 67% of them and GPT-4 87%.<Cite id="zhang2023snowball" />
  So an explanation the model writes after its answer defends the answer more than it checks it.
- **Check what can be checked with code**: that a cited case is in a court database, that a package
  is in the registry, that a number matches a tool's result (<ChapterRef to="tools" />).

</At>

<RecapCard>

- A model states a made-up answer as confidently as a true one, and nothing in the reply marks it.
- Asking several times catches many guesses, because real knowledge agrees with itself and guesses
  scatter. It misses things the model learned wrong, and a lower temperature only hides the
  scatter.
- Allowing "I don't know" helps, but tests that score it like a wrong answer teach models to guess.
- Made-up names, like software packages, can be registered by attackers. Check facts that matter
  with code, sources, or a person.

<At levels="deep">

- The entropy of the sampled answers measures the scatter. Answering beats "I don't know" when
  $p - (1-p)\,t/(1-t) > 0$, that is, when $p > t$.

</At>
</RecapCard>

**Next question:** a model can mislead you all on its own. But every tool result and every
helper's summary is text the harness pastes into a prompt: search results, web pages, files. What
if some of that text gives the model instructions of its own?
